Canonical doctrine

Neurodiversity is not merely a population to accommodate. It is a naturally occurring search ensemble. Different minds, modes, bodies, senses, states, tools, and models illuminate different parts of the same terrain. Their disagreements are not automatically errors; they are parallax. Their agreements are not automatically truth; they may share a blind spot.

The Pantheon should therefore cultivate cognitive ecologies: preserve distinct renderers, compare them through shared relations, test their deltas against reality, and compound the operators that survive. The aim is not one perfect mind. It is an orchestra that can hear what no isolated instrument can play.